{"actor_ref":"claude","class":"draft_history","document_uuid":"01a1a54a-308e-7244-af63-c971d182d919","id":"01a1a5d8-1da2-71e6-a9d2-848fda22e24a","idempotency_key":"e0a9920f0015f17227d127c7181050a277b180398c010af2625157482a9a9579","occurred_at":"2026-11-04T09:16:06Z","payload":{"note":"Session persistence. The server payload already carried the workspaces list (each with its boards and active_board index) and the active tab; what was missing was durability and the reset story. i-8df1: GET/PUT /api/session round-trips the whole Session JSON (workspaces, active, seq, tab) and the frontend already wrote to it with a debounce, so boards and the active board travel with the session rather than living only in the browser. i-0831: the client id that names the session was only in localStorage, so clearing site data silently orphaned the server copy - it is now mirrored in an rbc_client cookie and restored into localStorage on the next load, and the restore path already fell back to the server when localStorage had nothing. i-ba24: new GET /api/session/notice reports {reset, legacy_sessions} from a legacy_session_count() query, and the app shows a one-time banner ('...set aside as legacy_sessions') guarded by a localStorage flag, with the table kept for one release as recoverable. Evidence: cargo check --target wasm32-unknown-unknown passes; cdp_session.js -> AI_SESSION_PASS, which opens two workspace tabs through the strip and asserts they survive a reload (2 = 2 with the same titles), still survive after localStorage.clear() (2 = 2), that the client id is rebuilt from the cookie, and that with a seeded legacy_sessions row the notice appears on the first load and is gone on the next. Note on process: two earlier runs of that check reported PASS/FAIL on assertions that could not fail or could not match - the first compared 0 tabs to 0, the second regexed a string truncated before the word it looked for. Both were caught and fixed rather than accepted.","target":"t-9c43"},"type":"work.note_added","v":2}
